 discontinuous at both of the endpoints.","The function is constant on $[a,b]$.","The function is uniformly continuous on $[a,b]$.","The function is differentiable on $[a,b]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"106c7dd6fa6589a6bc872a8c","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_heine_cantor_theorem_a526e2fd_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does uniform continuity add beyond ordinary continuity?","options":["The function must stay bounded on the whole domain, even near the ends.","The function must be differentiable with a bounded derivative.","A single $\\varepsilon$ is fixed for the whole domain instead of each point.","A single scale of input control works across the whole domain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1cd6fcdd8240ee4b6205400e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_heine_cantor_theorem_a526e2fd_003_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"To which of the following does the Heine-Cantor theorem apply directly?","options":["$f(x)=1/x$ on $[1,2]$","$f(x)=1/x$ on $(0,1]$","$f(x)=x^2$ on $[0,\\infty)$","$f(x)=\\lfloor x\\rfloor$ on $[0,2]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"96bc400faf78a32e4079f795","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_heine_cantor_theorem_a526e2fd_014_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which example shows that in the Heine-Cantor theorem the closed interval $[a,b]$ cannot be replaced by an arbitrary bounded interval?","options":["$f(x)=1/x$ on $(0,1)$","$g(x)=x$ on $(0,1)$","$f(x)=x^2$ on $[0,\\infty)$","$f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ on $[0,1]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c0062efbfcce5370a5bae701","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_heine_cantor_theorem_a526e2fd_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=x^2$ on $[0,3]$, which is uniformly continuous by the Heine-Cantor theorem. Which rule for $\\delta$ guarantees, for every $\\varepsilon>0$, that $|f(x)-f(y)|<\\varepsilon$ for all $x,y\\in[0,3]$ with $|x-y|<\\delta$?","options":["$\\delta=\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3}$","$\\delta=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}$","$\\delta=\\frac{1}{6}$ for every $\\varepsilon$","$\\delta=\\frac{\\varepsilon}{6}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ece96e6db99d7e54aeaacdf2","time_limit":35}]}}